{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was shaped by the turbulent politics of the Crusader states. He inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Because of his youth a regency was established led by his mother and later by the King of Jerusalem. During this time Tripoli was under constant threat from Muslim forces and internal instability. Raymond spent much of his early reign learning the art of governance and military leadership under the watchful eyes of his guardians.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became known for his complex relationships with other Crusader leaders and neighboring Muslim rulers. He was often at odds with the King of Jerusalem and at times refused to support military campaigns. His rivalry with Baldwin IV of Jerusalem was especially notable. Raymond believed that the king was too young and too influenced by his advisors to make sound decisions. He even refused to recognize Baldwin IV as the rightful heir to the throne for a time. Despite this Raymond was also a skilled diplomat. He negotiated truces with Nur ad-Din and later with Saladin. These agreements brought temporary peace to the region and allowed Tripoli to recover from years of war. Raymond was known to be cautious and strategic preferring negotiation over reckless warfare.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nIn his later years Raymond played a crucial role in the defense of the Crusader states. He was present at the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army was decisively defeated by Saladin. Raymond escaped the battlefield but the loss of Jerusalem and much of the Holy Land followed. He died shortly after in 1187 possibly from illness or the stress of defeat. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV. Raymond III is remembered as a cautious but competent ruler who tried to preserve his lands amid overwhelming odds. His legacy is one of diplomacy and survival rather than glory. He was a key figure in the history of the Crusader states and his decisions had lasting consequences for the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "7", "logp